00:03:45.229 --> 00:03:46.229
PSI.

00:03:46.229 --> 00:03:52.299
So, you know, I don't think we have a true
understanding of what the actual number of

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:58.319
cases are for this infection, uh, because
a, it's a moving target and B, there's just

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:03.349
a lot of cases that haven't come to, uh, medical
attention or haven't been screened yet.

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:07.919
Uh, this is the kind of thing where at this
point in time as a February 26th, the more

00:04:07.919 --> 00:04:10.109
you'll look, the more you find.

00:04:10.109 --> 00:04:17.759
So, uh, and this is truly is a, a shifting
goalpost and a rapidly moving, uh, epidemics.

00:04:17.759 --> 00:04:23.449
So you know, those data are helpful, but they
don't tell the entire picture by any means

00:04:23.449 --> 00:04:26.650
in terms of how this is transmitted.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:32.540
Um, there's some confusion and there have
been conflicting claims made about both the

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:39.169
origin, the original transmission to a human
and then transmission between humans.

00:04:39.169 --> 00:04:42.490
So can you lay out what we know in terms of
how it's transmitted?

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:48.800
Uh, the, whether or not there has been asymptomatic
transmission and anything else that would

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.220
be relevant to know about this.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:56.389
Oh, I'm just going to roll up my sleeves here
now because this is a great topic.

00:04:56.389 --> 00:04:57.970
So a couple of things.

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:00.099
First of all, let's talk about how does humans
get this?

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:06.740
So this virus has likely existed for millennia
and, and existed in like, I have to be careful

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:07.740
with my words.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.930
It likely existed in bats.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.310
It was probably, again, careful with the words.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:18.090
It was probably transmitted from bats to another
nonhuman mammal host.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:23.740
And then it was probably transmitted from
that other nonhuman mammal host into a human.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.979
Um, that's probably what happened.

00:05:25.979 --> 00:05:30.539
And people that know much more about this
who are, you know, genomics and molecular

00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:34.970
biologists and geneticists can look at the
virus and look at the similarities of this

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:37.130
virus to other viruses and patterns of that.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:40.300
But that, that's probably the story.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.500
Of course, we know that it did jump into humans
and we started to hear about a growing number

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:48.699
of cases in Wu Han, China in, you know, mid
to late December.

00:05:48.699 --> 00:05:50.319
And of course we all know where we are now.

00:05:50.319 --> 00:05:55.110
We're sitting at the precipice of a, of a
pandemic where there's just wide-scale global

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:56.569
transmission of this infection.

00:05:56.569 --> 00:05:59.889
So how did we get it from there to here?

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:06.270
It's a respiratory virus and like other respiratory
viruses, it behaves in a very similar manner.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:11.139
People will have symptoms of a cough, they
can shed the virus through the respiratory

00:06:11.139 --> 00:06:16.650
tract and they can cough out the virus and
transmitted directly to people through coughing

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:19.940
and, and uh, and people in that close vicinity
of them.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:26.490
And also we know that with other respiratory
viruses in the droplets of, uh, which people

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:30.240
are coughing out, there can be virus in that
and that can land on surfaces.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.350
And you know, this virus might be able to
live for a period of time.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.180
Usually it's going to be in hours.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.970
Today's, although for this particular virus,
we don't have that answer yet.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.110
But in general it's hours, two days, depending
on the service, depending on the environment

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:45.110
it's in.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:48.520
And then if someone else touches that virus
and they know wipes, their eyes wipes their

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.460
nose, wipes, their mouth doesn't wash their
hands, they can get the infection that way.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:57.669
So that's how it's transmitted through direct
contact and through a likely through respiratory

00:06:57.669 --> 00:06:59.050
droplets.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:03.520
You touched on another really important point
and that's what's the deal with asymptomatic

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:04.830
transmission.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.050
It's a bit of a contentious issue.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:12.779
I mean there certainly have been some very
high profile reports of people who have had

00:07:12.779 --> 00:07:16.310
no symptoms whatsoever who are able to transmit
this infection.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:21.249
Now, in some cases when you scratch the surface
a little bit more, those people were actually

00:07:21.249 --> 00:07:24.999
symptomatic, just very mildly symptomatic.

00:07:24.999 --> 00:07:28.289
In other cases, they might have truly been
asymptomatic.

00:07:28.289 --> 00:07:33.561
But I think a more practical way to sort of
reframe this is rather than dealing with this,

00:07:33.561 --> 00:07:37.960
someone truly symptomatic or not, I like to
think of this in terms of, I like the term

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:39.729
subclinical.

00:07:39.729 --> 00:07:43.129
What subclinical means is someone has the
infection.

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:47.219
Someone might be shedding virus to it, you
know, either a lot or a little, but however

00:07:47.219 --> 00:07:51.460
sick they are, they're not sick enough that
they're going to seek medical care.

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:53.779
They're not sick enough where they say, you
know what, I'm really sick.

00:07:53.779 --> 00:07:56.879
I've got to stay home, or I got to seek, seek,
seek healthcare.

00:07:56.879 --> 00:08:01.279
So these are tend to be people with no symptoms
or very, very mild symptoms.

00:08:01.279 --> 00:08:05.840
And from some of the data that we've had from
this epidemic and actually some high quality

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:09.259
data emerging from China looking at thousands
and thousands of cases.

00:08:09.259 --> 00:08:14.009
There's a growing narrative and data to support
this narrative that many, many people have

00:08:14.009 --> 00:08:17.669
this who have this infection have actually
a mild course of illness.

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:21.120
So if people have such a mild course of illness
and there's a significant number of people

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.789
that have this infection that just aren't
sick enough to seek medical care, they are

00:08:25.789 --> 00:08:27.770
going to be transmitting this virus in the
community.

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:29.389
They're going to be transmitting it to other
people.

00:08:29.389 --> 00:08:34.259
And that's probably why we're seeing the numbers
that we're seeing now.

00:08:34.259 --> 00:08:40.440
How do you ultimately contain something like
this when it gets to a point like the one

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:41.500
that it is now?

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:45.880
In other words, do we have to, is it about
people not traveling around?

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:49.880
Is it really that, is it about, I mean just
what from a public health perspective, what

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:52.490
do we know about when it gets to this point,
how you deal with it?

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:57.570
Right, so containment is not an option.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.320
Containment is not an option.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:05.380
Now, earlier on in the epidemic, I've said
this a million times, I really first still

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:09.740
firmly believe it, early in the epidemic,
all efforts should have been made and were

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:11.670
made to contain this in China.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:15.930
But in the same sentence we were saying all
efforts should be made to contain this in

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:21.490
China, but we should be prepared for the scenario
that this cannot be contained in China.

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:23.360
And of course that's where we are now.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:28.250
And certainly those massive public health
initiatives in China were helpful.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:29.530
They were extraordinarily helpful.

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:34.310
I mean this impacted about 10% of the world's
population with, you know, mobility restrictions

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:35.310
and whatnot.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:38.029
Uh, and this really slowed the virus down.

00:09:38.029 --> 00:09:41.550
It's not going to stop it, it didn't stop
it, but it certainly slowed down the global

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:46.880
transmission by decreasing the number of exported
cases and decreasing transmission throughout

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:51.440
China, which was extremely helpful because
of probably bought the world, you know, four

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.560
or five ish weeks to prepare.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.420
And now the only hope is that people use that
time wisely and really scaled up their public

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:03.339
health systems, their healthcare systems,
their government structures that they use

00:10:03.339 --> 00:10:06.110
to deal with infections and epidemics.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:09.399
Because you know, it's not going to come to
anyone surprised that wherever you are on

00:10:09.399 --> 00:10:14.190
planet earth, you will likely be seeing more
cases than what you're seeing now.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:17.899
So in terms of an individual making travel
decisions, right?

00:10:17.899 --> 00:10:24.170
Um, sometimes we see and you start to see
China's got 78,000 cases in South Korea has

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:28.930
1200 or whatever, and people might start to
say, well, these are countries that I would

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:29.930
avoid.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:35.810
Now at the same time, if you consider influenza
standard flu in different places, you're not

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:39.370
looking at those numbers before you travel
necessarily.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:43.681
And you also have a risk of contracting flu
when you travel, depending on your age, it's

00:10:43.681 --> 00:10:46.470
not likely to kill you much like it seems.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:51.150
The death rate for Corona virus for those
under 40 is very, very low.

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.920
So how should the average person make a travel
decision around this?

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:59.529
Yeah, there's a few points I think that are
important to highlight here.

00:10:59.529 --> 00:11:07.279
Number one is everybody's going to have their
own risk aversion, tolerance threshold and,

00:11:07.279 --> 00:11:09.550
and of course we have to be humble here.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:12.100
We don't have all the answers to this infection.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:15.720
We have a lot more data than we did one and
two months ago, but we certainly don't have

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:21.720
all the answers here and we have to remain
humble that we, we, we just don't know some

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.190
things about this virus yet.

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:31.490
The next point is, uh, that people who ultimately
decide to travel should be very cautious about

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:35.670
travel advisories and travel restrictions.

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:42.430
Currently, currently as we have certain countries
that have more of a burden of a greater burden

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:44.040
of infection than others.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:48.230
We have travel advisories and travel restrictions
to those countries.

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:49.730
Every country is different.

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:54.370
Every country will have their own policy and
these are changing policies right now.

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:59.210
So if you're traveling, I think you have to
be mindful of the country you live in, the

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:04.250
country you're going to and any potential
stops along the way and what the current policies

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:05.899
are in those places.

00:12:05.899 --> 00:12:09.930
The second thing I think you have to be mindful
of is there's changing goalposts here.

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:16.740
I mean the who knows what policies might be
enacted or redacted between the time someone

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:20.250
books their ticket to the time someone actually
gets on the plane.

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:24.040
And so I think the third thing is if people
are choosing to travel, I mean I think you

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:31.079
should have some insurance or safety net in
place where for whatever reason, health, uh,

00:12:31.079 --> 00:12:36.199
you know, quarantine restrictions, cancellations,
you can get reimbursed for your flights or

00:12:36.199 --> 00:12:38.949
your, the money you spent on this trip.

00:12:38.949 --> 00:12:43.250
Last thing, when it comes to a vaccine, I've
seen reports that say a vaccine is eight months

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:44.250
away.

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:47.800
I've seen reports that say a vaccine in total
will take two years and that could be closer

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:55.130
to a year and a half away is my instinct is
if we are in a situation in 12 months where

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:59.310
we desperately need a vaccine, it will have
gotten very bad.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:00.310
Yeah.

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:09.750
I mean there's, the vaccine is, you know,
it takes time, energy, money and brains to

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:11.540
make an effective vaccine.

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:16.709
Uh, and people say, well, look at Ebola in
2014 and the West African epidemic, there

00:13:16.709 --> 00:13:19.000
was a deployment of a vaccine there.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:24.410
But remember, people had decades of effort
that went into the vaccine before it was deployed

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:26.210
in West Africa.

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:31.459
People started looking into a coronavirus
vaccine after SARS and then during Merz.

00:13:31.459 --> 00:13:34.730
But you know, these are underfunded, underappreciated
research endeavors.

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:38.899
And of course, now that we're on the verge
of a pandemic, everyone's thought, Oh, we

00:13:38.899 --> 00:13:40.269
should really reinvest in this.

00:13:40.269 --> 00:13:41.759
But it's, it's gonna take time.

00:13:41.759 --> 00:13:43.769
My bet is that it's certainly over a year.

00:13:43.769 --> 00:13:44.769
Now.

00:13:44.769 --> 00:13:50.550
The point is that if there's, this might be,
this might've run its course by then, in which

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:54.449
case one of the risks is that we just crossed
that off our budget line item.

00:13:54.449 --> 00:13:56.800
But this is not the last coronavirus we're
going to see.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:57.800
Right?

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:00.379
We had SARS, we had MERS, we have coven 19.

00:14:00.379 --> 00:14:01.379
This is going to happen again.

00:14:01.379 --> 00:14:04.110
You don't need a crystal ball to know that
this is going to happen again.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:08.560
So all efforts should really be placed on
a vaccine for either, hopefully, you know,

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:12.339
in a, in a perfect world would be available
for this epidemic or pandemic, whatever we

00:14:12.339 --> 00:14:13.339
want to call it.

00:14:13.339 --> 00:14:16.410
And certainly to prevent the next one

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:17.600
last thing very quickly.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:22.800
Is it, should we look forward to when it starts
to get a little warmer in certain parts of

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:23.800
the world?

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:27.259
Is it true that that when it gets warmer,
this is going to hopefully diminish in terms

00:14:27.259 --> 00:14:29.029
of how, how much it is spreading?

00:14:29.029 --> 00:14:32.529
I certainly hope so, but I'm not betting on
that whatsoever.

00:14:32.529 --> 00:14:38.449
And you know, we keep hearing that, you know,
Corona viruses, uh, don't do well in, in warmer

00:14:38.449 --> 00:14:39.449
climates.

00:14:39.449 --> 00:14:40.639
But I also think we have to.

00:14:40.639 --> 00:14:42.690
So the short answer is nobody knows.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:46.500
The longer answer is, you know, sometimes
during the course of an epidemic, a lot of

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.310
these rules don't apply.

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:56.339
So for example, influenza is typically, uh,
a winter, uh, uh, winter, uh, infection, but

00:14:56.339 --> 00:15:01.379
H one N one started in the summertime and
really spread pretty efficiently in the summertime

00:15:01.379 --> 00:15:02.850
and in the winter time.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:06.269
So you know, I'm not entirely sure if these
rules will apply to this.

00:15:06.269 --> 00:15:07.269
Of course.

00:15:07.269 --> 00:15:11.200
I hope it does, but uh, I don't think we can
bet on that and we shouldn't be betting on

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:12.200
that.

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:15.110
We should be prepared for an increased number
of cases regardless of what the temperature

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:16.110
is.

